436 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 214 0 R
/T (Game Organisation: Gather each group around its grid. Announce the context without revealing the objective: "You are a robot that must navigate this grid. Your mission: find a specific point. The problem? You don't know where it is! You will have to learn for yourselves by exploring." Give no information about the objective or the layout of obstacles. Pupils must discover for themselves, exactly like a machine that learns without prior knowledge.)
/E (Game Organisation: Gather each group around its grid. Announce the context without revealing the objective: "You are a robot that must navigate this grid. Your mission: find a specific point. The problem? You don't know where it is! You will have to learn for yourselves by exploring." Give no information about the objective or the layout of obstacles. Pupils must discover for themselves, exactly like a machine that learns without prior knowledge.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
437 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 214 0 R
/T (Each pupil receives a blank memorisation sheet \(empty grid or blank paper\). Simply explain: "You can use this sheet to note what you discover. It's your personal sheet, you can fill it in however you want. Warning: this sheet will be your only aid for programming your robot in the next part of the activity!" Exploration is collective \(the group decides movements together\), but each pupil fills in their own individual memorisation sheet. This allows observing the diversity of mental representations for the same lived experience.)
/E (Each pupil receives a blank memorisation sheet \(empty grid or blank paper\). Simply explain: "You can use this sheet to note what you discover. It's your personal sheet, you can fill it in however you want. Warning: this sheet will be your only aid for programming your robot in the next part of the activity!" Exploration is collective \(the group decides movements together\), but each pupil fills in their own individual memorisation sheet. This allows observing the diversity of mental representations for the same lived experience.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
