/S /H1
/P 220 0 R
/T (During observation, ask questions: How does the AI move at the beginning? \(Randomly, it even goes off the grid!\) Why does it make so many mistakes? \(It has no intuition, it tests EVERYTHING\) How many attempts does it need? \(Hundreds!\) Why does it continue after finding a path? \(It's looking for the optimal path, the shortest one\). Then lead a comparative discussion. Pupils found the path in a few attempts, the AI in 700. Pupils took several minutes, the AI less than a minute. Pupils think before acting, the AI tries everything without thinking. Pupils stay on the grid \(logical!\), the AI goes off the grid and tests everything. Pupils use their intuition, the AI only has calculations. Pupils look for "a path that works", the AI looks for "the optimal path". Pupils can explain their strategy, the AI cannot. Pupils adapt easily to a new problem, the AI must relearn everything from scratch.)
/E (During observation, ask questions: How does the AI move at the beginning? \(Randomly, it even goes off the grid!\) Why does it make so many mistakes? \(It has no intuition, it tests EVERYTHING\) How many attempts does it need? \(Hundreds!\) Why does it continue after finding a path? \(It's looking for the optimal path, the shortest one\). Then lead a comparative discussion. Pupils found the path in a few attempts, the AI in 700. Pupils took several minutes, the AI less than a minute. Pupils think before acting, the AI tries everything without thinking. Pupils stay on the grid \(logical!\), the AI goes off the grid and tests everything. Pupils use their intuition, the AI only has calculations. Pupils look for "a path that works", the AI looks for "the optimal path". Pupils can explain their strategy, the AI cannot. Pupils adapt easily to a new problem, the AI must relearn everything from scratch.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
563 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 220 0 R
/T (Pose discussion questions: Who learnt faster, you or the AI? \(It depends how you count! Fewer attempts for you, less time for the AI\) What are the AI's advantages? \(Doesn't get tired, very fast, finds the optimal solution, doesn't get discouraged\) What are YOUR advantages? \(Intuition, few errors, ability to explain, to adapt, to understand\) In which cases would the AI be better? And you?)
/E (Pose discussion questions: Who learnt faster, you or the AI? \(It depends how you count! Fewer attempts for you, less time for the AI\) What are the AI's advantages? \(Doesn't get tired, very fast, finds the optimal solution, doesn't get discouraged\) What are YOUR advantages? \(Intuition, few errors, ability to explain, to adapt, to understand\) In which cases would the AI be better? And you?)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
564 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 220 0 R
/T (Conclude on the richness of what they have experienced: "Today, you did much more than programme a robot. You discovered how YOU learn, you invented your own notation system, and you understood how machines are inspired by living beings' learning.". Open up to broader questions: Where do we find this type of learning in everyday life? \(GPS, video games, robot vacuum cleaners...\) Can robots truly "understand" or do they just calculate? For the written record, create together a poster or summary document with the different notation systems invented by the class, key words \(reinforcement learning, mental model, trial and error\), a comparative human/machine diagram, and pupils' reflections.)
/E (Conclude on the richness of what they have experienced: "Today, you did much more than programme a robot. You discovered how YOU learn, you invented your own notation system, and you understood how machines are inspired by living beings' learning.". Open up to broader questions: Where do we find this type of learning in everyday life? \(GPS, video games, robot vacuum cleaners...\) Can robots truly "understand" or do they just calculate? For the written record, create together a poster or summary document with the different notation systems invented by the class, key words \(reinforcement learning, mental model, trial and error\), a comparative human/machine diagram, and pupils' reflections.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
